==============================================================================
 PHASE 3 NOTES - GRAPH AND EXPLAINABLE PRIORITIES
==============================================================================

Goal of Phase 3:
  Phase 2 measured each file ALONE (lines, methods, complexity).
  Phase 3 looks at files TOGETHER: who imports whom (a graph), and
  then turns all measurements into an explainable review priority.

Slices:
  3A  Type index + explicit import graph (explicit-import-v1)     <- DONE (tests pass)
  3B  structural-v1 risk factors, the 59-point example, priority bands
  3C  Priority overrides + findings (HIGH_METHOD_COMPLEXITY, LARGE_FILE, ...)
  3D  CLI fixture report (the "credible fixture report" gate)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA 1 - A GRAPH IS JUST NODES AND ARROWS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Node  = one parsed Java file.
  Edge  = an ARROW  A -> B  meaning "file A imports a type declared in B".

  Direction matters. Read the arrow as "depends on":

      OrderController --> OrderService --> Order
             |                              ^
             +------------------------------+

  "Change Order.java. Who might be affected?"  Follow the arrows BACKWARDS:
  Order is imported by OrderService and OrderController. Those two are
  review candidates. (That backward walk is slice 3D/V1: impact candidates.)

  fan-out(A) = how many files A imports          (arrows leaving A)
  fan-in(B)  = how many files import B           (arrows arriving at B)

  In the drawing:  fan-out(OrderController) = 2   fan-in(Order) = 2
                   fan-out(Order) = 0             fan-in(OrderController) = 0

  Beginner mistake: mixing the two up. "Order has fan-in 2" means two files
  DEPEND ON Order. High fan-in = many things break if you change it.
  High fan-out = this file knows about many things (harder to understand).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA 2 - WHY A MAP (INDEX) INSTEAD OF COMPARING EVERY FILE WITH EVERY FILE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Naive plan: for every import in every file, look through every other
  file's declared types to find a match.
      1,000 files x 10 imports x 1,000 files = 10,000,000 comparisons.

  Index plan (what we built):
      Pass 1: Map<String, Set<String>>   "app.model.Order" -> {Order.java}
              one put per declared type.               cost ~ number of types
      Pass 2: for each import, ONE map lookup.          cost ~ number of imports

  A HashMap lookup does not get slower when the map grows (hash -> bucket).
  So total work grows with (types + imports), not (files x files).
  This is what "O(types) + O(imports)" means in Blueprint 9.6.

  Why the map value is a SET of files, not one file:
  two files could declare the same name (e.g. two copies of dup.Dup).
  Then the name is AMBIGUOUS. We refuse to guess and create no edge,
  but we remember the clash in ImportGraph.ambiguousTypes() as a warning.
  Mutation check (real): made the builder pick the first file instead ->
  test "do not pick the first match" failed. Restored.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA 3 - ADJACENCY LISTS (THE SAME EDGES, STORED FOR FAST QUESTIONS)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  ImportGraph keeps the edge list AND two maps built from it:

      outgoing:  A -> {B, C}     answers "what does A import?"    fan-out
      incoming:  B -> {A}        answers "who imports B?"         fan-in

  Each is Map<String, Set<String>> - a node, then the set of its neighbours.
  That is an "adjacency list". Two maps = twice the memory of one, but each
  question becomes one lookup instead of scanning all edges.
  Memory grows with (files + edges), which is small for any real repo.

  TreeMap / TreeSet everywhere -> sorted -> the same input always prints
  the same output (reproducibility, Blueprint 9.1). HashMap would work but
  its order can differ between runs.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA 4 - ONE IMPORT, ONE BUCKET (ImportEvidence)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Every import declaration goes into exactly ONE of five buckets:

    import static java.util.Map.entry;       -> static      (never resolved)
    import static java.util.Collections.*;   -> static      (static wins over wildcard)
    import java.util.*;                      -> wildcard    (never resolved)
    import app.model.Order;                  -> resolved    (one declaring file) -> edge
    import app.OrderService;  (own file)     -> resolved    but NO edge (self)
    import dup.Dup;  (declared in 2 files)   -> ambiguous   no edge, warning
    import java.util.List;                   -> unresolved/external
    import app.model.Order.Line;  (nested)   -> unresolved/external (MVP limit)
    import gone.Gone;  (file failed parse)   -> unresolved/external

  The record refuses to exist unless the five buckets add up to the total.
  So the report can always say "we saw 9 imports, 4 became evidence for
  edges, 5 we could not use" - honest about what the graph did NOT see.

  Why "unresolved OR external" is one bucket: without type information we
  cannot tell a JDK class from a library from a type in a file that failed
  to parse. They all look the same: "not in my index".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA 5 - PURE FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  ImportGraphBuilder.build(List<JavaFileAnalysis>) -> ImportGraph

  No file reading, no parser, no clock, no shared mutable state.
  Same list in, same graph out, every time. That is why it is easy to test
  (feed records, check edges) and safe to run anywhere (worker, test, CLI).
  The analyzer calls it once, AFTER the per-file loop, because the index
  needs every file's declared types before any import can be resolved.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 DUPLICATES AND SELF-EDGES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  import app.model.Order;   (line 2)
  import app.model.Order;   (line 4)   duplicate
  import app.model.Money;   (line 3)   same target file? no, Money.java

  -> OrderService -> Order   ONE edge, importedTypes [app.model.Order], lines [2, 4]
  -> OrderService -> Money   ONE edge
  If Order.java also declared Money, both imports would collapse into ONE
  edge with importedTypes [Money, Order]. Edge = file pair, not type pair.

  A file importing its own type resolves fine but creates no edge
  (an edge A -> A would say "A depends on A": true but useless).
  Mutation check (real): removed the self guard -> FileEdge threw
  "a file cannot depend on itself" in 3 tests. The record invariant caught
  the bug even before the assertions ran.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 REAL RUN - CODEPULSE ANALYZING ITSELF (2026-09-25)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd ~/Desktop/CodePulse/backend
  java -cp "target/classes:$(cat target/classpath.txt)" dev.codepulse.AnalyzeMain .

  java files: 60  parsed: 57  failed: 3   (the 3 broken fixtures)
  observed import edges (explicit-import-v1): 27
  import declarations: 316  resolved: 27  wildcard: 1  static: 41
                       ambiguous: 0  unresolved/external: 247

  Only 27 edges out of 316 imports! And ALL 27 come from the four harness
  classes in dev.codepulse importing dev.codepulse.engine classes.
  Every engine class has fan-in from the harnesses only, and fan-out 0.

  Why? All engine classes live in ONE package. Java lets same-package
  classes use each other WITHOUT an import line. JavaSourceAnalyzer uses
  SourceInventory, JavaSourceParser, MethodCollector ... and the graph sees
  none of it. This is blind spot #1 from Blueprint 9.6, in our own code.

  The 41 static imports are the tests' assertEquals/assertTrue.
  The 247 unresolved are JDK (java.util...), JavaParser, and JUnit types.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 THREE THINGS THE GRAPH CANNOT SEE (gate question - know these cold)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. Same-package references (no import line is written)   <- biggest one
  2. Wildcard imports (import a.b.*;) and static imports
  3. Fully qualified names used inline (new a.b.C()), nested types,
     reflection (Class.forName), Spring dependency injection, XML config

  So fan-in / fan-out are LOWER BOUNDS. Always say "observed imports",
  never "all dependencies", never "call graph".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 NULL vs 0 IN THIS SLICE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  PARSED file that imports nothing        -> fan-out 0   (measured: nothing)
  PARSE_FAILED file                       -> fan-out null (not a node; not measured)
  evidence(path) for a failed file        -> null
  parsed file with zero imports           -> ImportEvidence.NONE (all zeros)
